  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nIt is a quiet and mild morning with areas of light fog\ndeveloping. Visibility is holding in the 3 to 5 mile range, but a\nfew areas have dropped to around a mile. High clouds streaming\neast from the Dakotas may improve the fog or at least hinder\nadditional development. A weak shortwave rounding the crest of \nthe ridge, still centered over the southern Rockies, will \nprogress across the Upper Midwest later this morning and\nafternoon. An uncapped and unstable atmosphere may allow for a \ncouple brief storms to develop during peak heating. Otherwise,\nit will be another hot day with highs in the upper 80s to lower\n90s. \n\nA far stronger shortwave will progress east across the northern\nPlains Thursday and reach the Upper Midwest Thursday night,\nbecoming more amplified as it does so. There remains quite a bit\nof spread with the development of a surface low center from\neastern SD to KS, but clustering has tightened with the EPS and\nAIFS focusing on eastern SD, to southern MN or northern IA, to \nsoutheastern IA through Friday. Very rich moisture will stream \nnorth on a 35 kt LLJ and frontogenesis near the low track will \nfocus an area of heavy rainfall just north of the low. At least\na few inches of rain appear probable in this corridor, but \ngiven the latitudinal spread in the guidance, confidence remains\nlow with spatial details. Some embedded thunderstorms are \nlikely, but very poor lapse rates will lead to meager instability\nand limit the severe risk.\n\nThe system will exit to the east by Friday night and the weekend\nlooks dry with near normal temperatures. A deep trough will dig\ninto the Pacific Northwest during this time, which will buckle\nthe ridge back northeast Sunday and Monday. Temperatures will\ntrend back up, but surface high pressure over the Great Lakes\nshould keep the most intense heat off to the west. A weakening\ncold front associated with the PacNW trough may bring another\nshot of showers and storms the Monday night-Tuesday timeframe.",
